// President — stress-test DSL encoding (NOT part of the canonical corpus).
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/climbing/president.html (Pagat), plus the
// page's "Number of ranks" section for the 4-player extended exchange
// (President<->Scum swap two cards, Vice-President<->Vice-Scum swap one).
//
// 4 players, standard 52-card deck, no jokers, suits irrelevant. Ranking
// (high to low): 2 A K Q J 10 9 8 7 6 5 4 3. A play is a single card or a
// same-rank set of 2 or 3; a set is beaten only by a higher-ranked set of
// the SAME size, or passed. A trick clears when action returns to the last
// player to play (everyone else passed); that player leads next. A player
// who runs out of cards is simply skipped from then on — the hand
// continues among the rest. Finishing order over the whole hand gives the
// four social ranks: President (1st out), Vice-President (2nd), Vice-Scum
// (3rd), Scum (last, and never has to finish shedding). Before the NEXT
// hand's play (after the new deal): Scum gives President their two highest
// cards and President gives back any two of their choice; Vice-Scum gives
// Vice-President their one highest card and Vice-President gives back any
// one. Roles, once established, persist and set the following hand's
// leader (President leads first) and this whole exchange.
//
// === Simplifications / deliberate cuts (declared, not hidden) ===
//
// 1. Combination engine reuse (GAP, major — see inline `// GAP:` at the
//    `round climb` site). The kernel's only combination-engine queries
//    usable by `round climb` are `bigtwo_lead_options` / `bigtwo_follows`
//    (cardlang/stdlib/functions.py's STDLIB_CLIMB_LEADS/FOLLOWS are a fixed,
//    Python-backed frozenset the checker validates against; adding a
//    `president_lead_options`/`president_follows` pair would require writing
//    Python, which this stress test forbids). Reusing Big Two's engine as-is
//    is a genuine rules distortion in two ways: (a) it breaks ties on equal
//    rank by suit (spades > hearts > clubs > diamonds), so e.g. a single 7 of
//    spades can legally "beat" a single 7 of hearts — President's own rule
//    text says "Suits are irrelevant" and equal rank never beats equal rank;
//    (b) it also offers Big Two's five-card combinations (straights,
//    flushes, full houses, quads) whenever a hand happens to hold one —
//    President has no such combinations at all (singles/pairs/triples
//    only). The climbing `round` form does not run `active_rules` over its
//    move type (confirmed by reading cardlang/runtime/mechanics.py's
//    ClimbForm — legality comes entirely from the named `combinations`/
//    `follows` queries, with no rule-cascade hook to narrow them), so there
//    is no DSL-level way to filter either distortion out. `opened : Boolean
//    = true` (never set false) neutralizes the other Big-Two-specific
//    behaviour baked into `bigtwo_lead_options` — the forced 3-of-diamonds
//    opening lead — which President has no equivalent of, so suppressing it
//    is the *correct* adaptation, not a workaround.
// 2. First hand's leader: Pagat's base rules don't say who leads before any
//    roles exist. Seat 0 leads hand 1; every later hand's leader is that
//    hand's President (Pagat: "The President leads the new hand").
// 3. Physical reseating ("the President selects the most comfortable
//    chair...") is cosmetic and never affects legality in this engine (seats
//    are fixed identities; only *who leads* matters), so it is omitted —
//    President leading is the only seating effect modeled.
// 4. Finish-order tie-break within one trick (GAP, minor/major depending on
//    frequency — see inline `// GAP:` at the tracking loop). If two players
//    both empty their hand inside the same trick (e.g. one sheds their last
//    card and the very next player beats it with their own last card),
//    `round climb` returns only the trick's final `outcome`; the surrounding
//    body has no visibility into which of the two emptied first. Finish
//    order between simultaneous shedders is assigned by ascending seat
//    order, not true within-trick chronology.
// 5. No numeric score exists in the source rules (finishing order is a
//    purely social result, replayed hand after hand). To give this
//    simulation a terminating `winner:`, hands are played until
//    `hands_played >= 6` and each hand awards invented points by finish
//    rank (President 3, Vice-President 2, Vice-Scum 1, Scum 0); highest
//    total wins. This whole scoring scheme is this file's invention, not
//    Pagat's.
// 6. Revolutions, bombs, and other special-card variants are out of scope
//    per the brief; only the base climbing/passing rules are modeled.
// 7. (Language finding, fixed here, not a cut.) There is no primitive for
//    "give your top K cards" as one deterministic multi-card movement.
//    The obvious encoding — compute a threshold T such that at least K
//    cards rank at or above T, then `move chosen K cards where rank_value
//    >= T` — is WRONG whenever K > 1 and the K-th place is a tie that
//    extends the qualifying pool past K: e.g. a hand of [2, 10, 10, 7, 3]
//    (rank_values 15,10,10,7,3), giving the top 2, computes T=10 (10 is the
//    lowest value with >= 2 cards at or above it), so the pool is {2, 10,
//    10} — three cards — and a free `chosen 2` from that pool can legally
//    pick {10, 10}, illegally keeping the single highest card (the 2). A
//    forced top-K give is therefore expressed as K sequential forced
//    top-1 gives (below), each recomputing the threshold against the
//    shrunken hand — for K=1 the pool is always exactly the tied top rank,
//    so the residual "choice" is only ever between suit-equivalent
//    duplicates, which is genuinely harmless (suit never matters to rank
//    comparison in this game).
// 8. (Runtime finding, fixed here, not a cut.) `round climb`'s own
//    `ClimbForm.__init__` asserts the named leader is a current participant
//    (`hand[player] is not empty`) — a bare `leader := outcome` crashes the
//    next trick whenever the trick's winner sheds out on their own winning
//    play (a normal event in President, impossible in Big Two, where the
//    whole hand ends the instant anyone sheds, so the leader is never asked
//    to lead again). Worked around below by falling forward, in seating
//    order, to the next player who still holds cards.

game President {

  players: 4
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52
  ranking: 2 A K Q J 10 9 8 7 6 5 4 3

  zones {
    deck         : Deck
    hand[player] : Hand<player>
    trick_pile   : TrickPile          // the climbing trick in progress
    discard      : Discard            // spent tricks (no capture in President)
  }

  state {
    // Persist across hands: the four social ranks, and the invented match score.
    president     : Player? = none
    vice          : Player? = none
    vice_scum     : Player? = none
    scum          : Player? = none
    points[player] : Integer = 0
    hands_played  : Integer = 0
    // Neutralizes bigtwo_lead_options's Big-Two-specific 3-of-diamonds forced
    // opening lead (see simplification 1 above) — President has no such rule.
    opened        : Boolean = true
  }

  phase hand_sequence repeats until hands_played >= 6 {
    state {
      leader   : Player? = none
      // Per-hand finishing-order tracking (hand_sequence-scoped, not
      // phase-play-scoped, so `phase scoring` — a sibling of `phase play` —
      // can still read it once play exits).
      finish1  : Player? = none   // 1st out: next President
      finish2  : Player? = none   // 2nd out: next Vice-President
      finish3  : Player? = none   // 3rd out: next Vice-Scum
      finish4  : Player? = none   // last: next Scum
      recorded[player] : Boolean = false
    }

    before_each {
      move all cards to deck
      shuffle deck
      deal 13 cards from deck to each hand
      hands_played += 1
      leader := (if president is none then 0 else president)
      finish1 := none
      finish2 := none
      finish3 := none
      finish4 := none
      for each player p: recorded[p] := false
    }

    // The inter-hand exchange (Pagat: "When the deal is complete..."). Only
    // runs from the second hand on, once roles exist. Scum's and Vice-Scum's
    // gives are forced to their highest card(s); President's and
    // Vice-President's returns are the giver's free choice — written out
    // explicitly (not "each player simultaneously") since the four legs are
    // asymmetric in both direction and cardinality.
    phase exchange when president is not none {
      legal_moves: [transfer_between_hands]

      // Scum's two highest, given as two sequential forced single-highest
      // gives, not one "chosen 2 cards where rank_value >= threshold" move —
      // see the header note on why a single combined threshold-pool grab is
      // wrong when N > 1.
      for each player p: if p == scum {
        let t1 = highest_threshold(p)
        move chosen 1 cards from hand[p] where c => rank_value(c) >= t1 to hand[president]
        let t2 = highest_threshold(p)
        move chosen 1 cards from hand[p] where c => rank_value(c) >= t2 to hand[president]
      }
      for each player p: if p == president {
        transfer chosen 2 cards from hand[p] to hand[scum]
      }
      for each player p: if p == vice_scum {
        let threshold = highest_threshold(p)
        move chosen 1 cards from hand[p] where c => rank_value(c) >= threshold to hand[vice]
      }
      for each player p: if p == vice {
        transfer chosen 1 cards from hand[p] to hand[vice_scum]
      }
    }

    phase play {
      legal_moves: [play_combination]

      // One climbing trick per `round climb`, repeated until at most one
      // player still has an unrecorded finish (i.e. still holds cards) —
      // that lone survivor is Scum by elimination, never forced to keep
      // playing (Pagat: only the loser is left "with any cards").
      repeat until (number of players where not recorded[player]) <= 1 {
        // GAP (major): `combinations`/`follows` can only name
        // `bigtwo_lead_options`/`bigtwo_follows` — see simplification 1
        // above. `until false` (a required clause) never ends the trick
        // early: unlike Big Two, going out mid-trick must NOT end the
        // trick — the rest of the ring still gets to try to beat the
        // shedding play (round climb's own `next_actor` already skips a
        // player the moment their hand is empty, with no chooser draw, so
        // this falls out for free once `until` never fires).
        round climb play_combination from leader
              over players where hand[player] is not empty
              source hand into trick_pile
              combinations bigtwo_lead_options follows bigtwo_follows
              until false
        move all cards from trick_pile to discard
        // The trick's last player normally leads next — but if that player
        // just went out on their winning play (round climb's own
        // `next_actor` already let them win while empty), they cannot lead:
        // the next surviving player in seating order does instead. Fixed to
        // three hops since there are only four seats — enough to reach
        // whichever of the other three still holds cards.
        leader := (
          if hand[outcome] is not empty then outcome
          elif hand[outcome offset_by left] is not empty then outcome offset_by left
          elif hand[outcome offset_by left offset_by left] is not empty
            then outcome offset_by left offset_by left
          else outcome offset_by left offset_by left offset_by left
        )

        // GAP (minor/major): if two players both empty their hand within
        // this one trick, this loop cannot see which one emptied first —
        // `round climb` exposes only the trick's outcome, not per-play
        // timing. They are recorded in ascending seat order instead.
        for each player p:
          if hand[p] is empty and not recorded[p] {
            recorded[p] := true
            if finish1 is none {
              finish1 := p
            } else {
              if finish2 is none {
                finish2 := p
              } else {
                if finish3 is none {
                  finish3 := p
                } else {
                  finish4 := p
                }
              }
            }
          }
      }
      // The lone player left holding cards (if any) is Scum by elimination.
      for each player p: if not recorded[p] {
        finish4 := p
        recorded[p] := true
      }
    }

    phase scoring {
      president := finish1
      vice      := finish2
      vice_scum := finish3
      scum      := finish4
      points[finish1] += 3
      points[finish2] += 2
      points[finish3] += 1
    }
  }

  winner: highest points
}

// === President-local stdlib-adjacent helpers ===

// A hand's highest rank_value. `chosen 1 cards where rank_value(c) >=
// highest_threshold(p)` always has a nonempty pool (every hand has a
// highest card) and the pool is exactly that top rank's tier — so the
// "choice" is only ever between suit-equivalent duplicates of the single
// highest rank, never a real decision.
function highest_threshold(p : Player) = max over hand[p] as c: rank_value(c)
